    "The directive '#FLUX -C hostname:cocoflops-hgx-1' hardcodes the job to a single, specific node. This is generally an anti-pattern in shared computing environments as it prevents the scheduler from flexibly placing the job on any available node that meets the requirements. If 'cocoflops-hgx-1' is busy, offline, or removed from the cluster, the job will fail to run or remain queued indefinitely. It is better to request generic features and let the scheduler find a suitable node.",
    "The script requests 48 CPUs but launches a `torchrun` command with only 2 processes (`--nproc_per_node=2`). This means that, at a minimum, 46 of the allocated CPU cores will be completely idle for the entire job duration, representing a significant waste of resources. The `--cpus-per-task` request should be reduced to better match the application's needs, or the application should be configured to utilize more threads for data loading to take advantage of the allocated CPUs."
